<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Dawud Pasha</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1767–1851</span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Davud Pasha of Baghdad</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Dawud Pasha was the last <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mamluk">Mamluk</a> governor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baghdad">Baghdad</a>, ruling from 1817 to 1831. A Georgian-born former slave, he built factories, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/printing-press">printing</a> works and a modern-style army, and dealt as an equal with the British. When he defied <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sultan">Sultan</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahmud-ii">Mahmud II</a>, an Ottoman army, helped by plague and floods, took Baghdad in 1831 and ended Mamluk rule in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iraq">Iraq</a>.</p></div>
